# grep — real-world examples

## Search a file for a pattern

```sh
grep pattern file
```

## Case-insensitive search with line numbers

```sh
grep -in pattern file
```

## Recursive search through a folder

```sh
grep -R pattern folder
```

## Read search patterns from a file (one per line)

```sh
grep -f pattern_file file
```

## Invert: lines NOT containing the pattern

```sh
grep -v pattern file
```

## Whole-word match only

```sh
grep -w file somefile    # matches "file", not "files"
```

## Regular expressions

```sh
grep "^00" file    # lines starting with 00
grep -E "[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}\.[0-9]{1,3}" file    # find IP addresses
```

## Recursive whole-word search showing file:line

```sh
grep -rnw 'directory' -e "pattern"
```

## Exclude grep itself from ps output (bracket trick)

```sh
ps aux | grep '[h]ttpd'    # [] on the first letter stops self-matching
```

## Highlight matches but keep all other lines

```sh
ps aux | grep -E --color 'bash|$'
```

## Context lines around a match

```sh
grep -A4 "pattern" file1   # 4 lines after
grep -B4 "pattern" file1   # 4 lines before
```

## Multiple patterns with extended regex (egrep = grep -E)

```sh
grep -Ei 'hello.*world' file1        # hello followed by world
grep -Ei 'hello|world' file1         # hello OR world
grep -Eiv 'hello|world' file1        # lines with neither
grep -Er 'hello|world|friend' file*  # OR across files
```

## Literal match, regex disabled (fgrep = grep -F)

```sh
grep -F 'hello$' file1    # finds the literal string hello$
```

## Strip comments and blank lines from a config file

```sh
grep -v '^#\|^$' /etc/some/conf/file
grep -v -e '^ *#' -e '^$' /etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf
```

## Homelab: hunt errors in logs and service output

```sh
grep -i error /var/log/messages | tail -20
journalctl -u plexmediaserver --no-pager | grep -iE 'fail|error'
grep -rn 'ExecStart' /etc/systemd/system/
```

| Flag | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| -i | ignore case |
| -n | show line numbers |
| -r / -R | recurse into directories |
| -v | invert match |
| -w | whole words only |
| -E | extended regex (egrep) |
| -F | literal strings (fgrep) |
| -f FILE | patterns from a file |
| -A/-B/-C N | context after / before / both |
| -l | list matching filenames only |
| -c | count matching lines |
